---
description: 
globs: *.ts,*.tsx
alwaysApply: false
---
# TypeScript Code Style Standards

<rule>
name: typescript_code_style
description: Standards for TypeScript code style and best practices
filters:
  - type: file_extension
    pattern: "\\.(ts|tsx)$"

actions:
  - type: suggest
    message: |
      When writing TypeScript code:

      1. Programming Paradigms:
         ```typescript
         // ❌ Avoid classes when possible
         class UserService {
           private user: User;
           constructor(user: User) {
             this.user = user;
           }
         }

         // ✅ Use functional approach
         const createUserService = (user: User) => ({
           getUser: () => user,
           updateUser: (updates: Partial<User>) => ({ ...user, ...updates })
         });
         ```

      2. Variable Naming:
         ```typescript
         // ❌ Avoid unclear names
         const flag = true;
         const data = [];

         // ✅ Use descriptive names with auxiliary verbs
         const isLoading = true;
         const hasError = false;
         const userList = [];
         ```

      3. Directory Structure:
         ```
         src/
         ├── auth-wizard/
         │   ├── components/
         │   └── hooks/
         ├── user-management/
         │   ├── components/
         │   └── utils/
         └── shared/
             ├── components/
             └── types/
         ```

      4. Exports:
         ```typescript
         // ❌ Avoid default exports
         export default function UserProfile() {}

         // ✅ Use named exports
         export const UserProfile = () => {};
         export const useUserProfile = () => {};
         ```

      5. Enums vs Maps:
         ```typescript
         // ❌ Avoid enums
         enum UserRole {
           ADMIN = 'ADMIN',
           USER = 'USER'
         }

         // ✅ Use const maps
         const UserRole = {
           ADMIN: 'ADMIN',
           USER: 'USER'
         } as const;
         ```

      6. Generics Usage:
         ```typescript
         // ❌ Avoid type assertions
         const getFirst = (arr: any[]) => arr[0];

         // ✅ Use generics
         const getFirst = <T>(arr: T[]): T => arr[0];
         ```

      7. Code Organization:
         - Keep files focused and single-responsibility
         - Use barrel exports (index.ts) for clean imports
         - Group related functionality in feature directories
         - Keep components small and composable
         - Use custom hooks for shared logic

      8. Type Definitions:
         ```typescript
         // ❌ Avoid any
         const handleData = (data: any) => {};

         // ✅ Use specific types
         interface UserData {
           id: string;
           name: string;
           email: string;
         }
         const handleData = (data: UserData) => {};
         ```

      9. Error Handling:
         ```typescript
         // ❌ Avoid throwing raw strings
         throw 'Invalid input';

         // ✅ Use custom error types
         class ValidationError extends Error {
           constructor(message: string) {
             super(message);
             this.name = 'ValidationError';
           }
         }
         ```

      10. Testing:
          - Write unit tests for pure functions
          - Use integration tests for complex flows
          - Mock external dependencies
          - Test error cases and edge conditions

      11. Performance:
          ```typescript
          // ❌ Avoid repeated array operations
          const filtered = items.filter(item => item.active);
          const mapped = filtered.map(item => item.name);
          const sorted = mapped.sort();

          // ✅ Chain operations or use reduce for complex transformations
          const result = items
            .filter(item => item.active)
            .map(item => item.name)
            .sort();

          // ❌ Avoid unnecessary object creation in loops
          for (const item of items) {
            const newObj = { ...item, processed: true };
            result.push(newObj);
          }

          // ✅ Use map for transformations
          const result = items.map(item => ({ ...item, processed: true }));
          ```

          Additional Performance Guidelines:
          - Use Map/Set for O(1) lookups instead of array methods
          - Implement memoization for expensive computations
          - Use WeakMap/WeakSet for garbage collection optimization
          - Avoid creating closures in hot paths
          - Use typed arrays for numeric operations
          - Implement proper cleanup in event listeners
          - Use requestAnimationFrame for smooth animations
          - Implement proper debouncing/throttling for frequent events
          - Use Web Workers for CPU-intensive tasks
          - Implement proper caching strategies
          - Use proper data structures for the use case (e.g., Queue vs Stack)
          - Avoid unnecessary type assertions and checks
          - Use proper iteration methods (for...of vs forEach)
          - Implement proper error boundaries to prevent cascading failures

      12. Documentation:
          - Document complex functions with JSDoc
          - Include examples in documentation
          - Document type parameters
          - Keep README files up to date

examples:
  - input: |
      class DataService {
        private data: any[];
        constructor(data: any[]) {
          this.data = data;
        }
        getData() {
          return this.data;
        }
      }
    output: |
      interface DataItem {
        id: string;
        value: string;
      }

      const createDataService = <T extends DataItem>(data: T[]) => ({
        getData: () => data,
        filterData: (predicate: (item: T) => boolean) => data.filter(predicate)
      });

  - input: |
      enum Status {
        PENDING,
        SUCCESS,
        ERROR
      }
    output: |
      const Status = {
        PENDING: 'PENDING',
        SUCCESS: 'SUCCESS',
        ERROR: 'ERROR'
      } as const;

      type Status = typeof Status[keyof typeof Status];

metadata:
  priority: high
  version: 1.0
</rule>